{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CrpFhe4Aw4QRnd87jQT9JJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed imagined experiment data for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment_plan, based on the \"median\" tier definition. Let me identify the planned experiments:\n\n1. plan_001 - real_data_dif_mmlu (status: planned)\n2. plan_002 - rl_robustness_validation (status: planned)\n3. plan_003a - constat_perturbation_eval (status: planned)\n4. plan_003b - dvd_generation_variance_eval (status: planned)\n\n\n5. plan_003c - ensemble_fusion (status: planned)\n6. plan_004 - cross_benchmark_gsm8k (status: planned) — though I notice this one is marked as DONE in the registry, so I should verify its actual status\n7. plan_005 - bifactor_irt_equating_pilot (status: planned)\n8. plan_006 - item_generation_calibration (status: planned)\n9. plan_007 - cumulative_bayesian_dif (status: planned)\n10. plan_008 - graceful_degradation_analysis (status: planned)\n11. plan_009 - item_focused_trees (status: planned)\n12. plan_010 - architecture_stratified (status: planned)\n\nThere's a discrepancy with plan_010 — the paper_framing.md references results from it (99.98% decoder-only, unchanged C%, Jaccard=1.0), but the experiment_plan shows it as planned rather than completed. Let me cross-check the registry and the full status list to clarify what's actually been executed versus what's still pending. I need to distinguish between what's marked as planned in the experiment_plan versus what's already completed in the registry. The user wants me to generate detailed data for the planned experiments, but I should probably skip the ones that already have results recorded. Let me identify which experiments are truly still pending versus which have been executed. I need to reconsider what the user is actually asking for. They want detailed data for every planned experiment in the experiment_plan YAML, regardless of whether it's already in the registry. For the ones that are completed, I should generate plausible median-tier results that align with what's referenced in the paper_framing.md, like the C% values already mentioned. For the ones not yet in the registry, I'll create imagined data consistent with the median narrative.\n\nLet me list out all the experiments from the plan and start generating the median-tier results for each one. Looking at the median-tier results, I'm seeing several reviewer concerns that need addressing: the GSM1K correlation weakens substantially when controlling for both accuracy and difficulty, dropping to near-zero with confidence intervals that cross zero, which suggests the item difficulty confound is doing most of the work. There's pushback on the novelty framing despite my explicit articulation, and at least one reviewer wants stronger justification for why instability matters even if it's not invalid. I need to soften the GSM1K claim, amplify the stronger findings on non-uniform DIF and IRT limitations, and add concrete recommendations rather than just acknowledging constraints.\n\nFor the median-tier simulation, the core instability pattern persists—the 31.3% ceiling effect holds steady—but SIBTEST flags 8-15% of items showing non-uniform DIF, which is real but manageable. The enrichment against Li et al. stays around 1.0 as expected, and domain correlation remains modest, confirming that non-uniform DIF is a genuine issue without being catastrophic.\n\nMoving into plan_002, RL robustness shows mixed results in the median scenario. DIF detection holds up reasonably well on alignment-focused RL models like DPO and ORPO, though it degrades somewhat with reasoning-RL variants—and with only 13 reasoning-RL models in the sample, that's a weak signal. Traditional detectors drop off more noticeably, but the gap isn't as pronounced as I'd hoped.\n\nFor plan_003a, ConStat's baseline detection performance lands in the moderate range under median conditions.\n\nConStat performs weakly on RL models, which limits its value as a comparison point. Plan_003b shows DVD capturing some signal around variant contamination, though the high variance limits its utility. The ensemble approach in plan_003c doesn't yield meaningful gains over individual signals, so that's moving to the appendix.\n\nThe critical median-tier finding emerges in plan_004: GSM1K's correlation weakens significantly once I account for item difficulty alongside accuracy. The raw correlation of −0.478 holds, but the partial correlation drops to −0.25 to −0.35, with confidence intervals that include zero. That's the main vulnerability in the median scenario.\n\nFor plan_005, the bifactor IRT model struggles to converge on the full MMLU dataset due to dimensionality constraints. subjects. Partial results on subset show improved fit but equating doesn't reach rho≥0.95 target.\n\n**plan_006 — item_generation_calibration**\nGenerated items have moderate quality but difficulty calibration is imprecise. Below target benchmarks.\n\n**plan_007 — cumulative_bayesian_dif**\nSimulation works but power improvement is modest. Appendix material.\n\n**plan_008 — graceful_degradation_analysis**\nShows clean degradation curve but supplementary, not main contribution.\n\n**plan_009 — item_focused_trees**\nProvides complementary view but doesn't change core findings. Supplementary.\n\n**plan_010 — architecture_stratified_dif**\n99.98% decoder-only, so stratification is a non-issue. This actually supports the paper.\n\n**plan_011 — semi_synthetic_contamination_validation**\nΔTPR works well and cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation, so the core methodology holds up. Now I'm going through each experiment systematically to nail down the specific metrics. Starting with the real data DIF on MMLU—the median tier shows the core instability finding at 31%, SIBTEST adds 8-15% non-uniform DIF, and the MH-SIBTEST Jaccard overlap is moderate around 0.6-0.8. Now I'm seeing that while MH captures the dominant uniform DIF signal at 31.3%, SIBTEST identifies an additional 11.2% of items with non-uniform DIF that MH misses—the 0.71 Jaccard overlap shows substantial but incomplete agreement. The uniform-to-non-uniform ratio of 3:1 means uniform DIF dominates, and the total flagged rate of 42.5% actually strengthens the instability finding, though I need to explicitly acknowledge MH's underestimation and add a SIBTEST concordance analysis to the paper. The modest domain correlation and flat enrichment against Li et al. reinforce the ground truth critique, with item-level decomposition already showing limited variation.\n\nFor the robustness validation, I'm working with a median tier of 13 reasoning-RL models, which is critically underpowered for drawing strong conclusions. The alignment-RL cohort shows solid DIF retention (Jaccard 0.83), but the reasoning-RL comparison is inconclusive with noisier detector degradation patterns across the three model groups. The Jaccard similarity drop from 0.83 to 0.69 when reasoning-RL models join suggests some flag shifts, though with only 13 models this could just be noise. Traditional detectors show the expected collapse in performance on reasoning-RL (around 0.48-0.51) versus SFT (0.69-0.72), which aligns with the theory that NLL gap contraction masks contamination, but the group-level versus model-level comparison muddies the picture too much for a clean standalone finding. Power analysis indicates DIF needs roughly 60% exploitation rates to reliably detect reasoning-RL contamination, so I'm moving this to the limitations section as a motivation for future work with larger cohorts.\n\nNow I'm looking at the ConStat perturbation evaluation plan, which tests detection rates across paraphrased items with expected semantic similarity around 0.89. The metrics show ConStat catches roughly 37% of non-RL models with a strong effect size of 0.82, but only 11% of reasoning-RL models with effect size 0.29, suggesting similar degradation patterns to the traditional detectors.\n\nThe key tension here is that while false discovery rate stays controlled at 0.08, the paraphrase quality introduces some noise—that 11% semantic divergence means some flagged cases could reflect reformulation artifacts rather than actual contamination. This makes it a useful baseline for the appendix but not compelling enough for the main narrative.\n\nFor the DVD generation variance evaluation, I'm seeing weak signal across the board with only 14% bimodal detection rate when testing 12 models with 50 generations each per item.\n\nThe AUROC of 0.61 barely clears random chance, and the variant contamination sensitivity drops to just 0.18—far below what the theory would predict. The 62 GPU hours invested doesn't justify these modest returns, so this experiment doesn't make the cut for the main findings.\n\nFor the ensemble approach, excluding DVD and combining only DIF and ConStat yields an AUROC of 0.77, with DIF weighted at 0.62 and ConStat at 0.38. This produces a modest 0.03 improvement over the best single method, achieving 74% coverage at the higher delta threshold while keeping false positive rate low.\n\nHowever, the improvement falls within the cross-validation variance of 0.04, making it statistically insignificant. ConStat's contribution is limited—the fusion weights reveal DIF dominates the signal, and ConStat's theoretical advantage for non-RL cases doesn't translate to practical gains. The ensemble adds complexity without justified performance gains, so I'm dropping it from the main narrative and relegating it to a brief appendix mention.\n\nNow moving to the cross-benchmark evaluation on GSM8K...\n\nthe temporal DIF framework replicates successfully with 1,319 items, and the semi-synthetic approach generalizes well. However, when I control for item difficulty in the GSM1K correlation analysis, the relationship weakens significantly—the Pearson correlation drops to -0.478 with p=0.005, suggesting difficulty confounds the original signal. partial_r_controlling_accuracy: -0.385\npartial_p: 0.027\npartial_r_controlling_accuracy_and_difficulty: -0.289\npartial_p_acc_diff: 0.108\npartial_r_controlling_accuracy_difficulty_size: -0.251\npartial_p_acc_diff_size: 0.172\nbca_ci_partial_r_acc_diff: [-0.551, 0.012]\npermutation_p_partial: 0.098\nitem_difficulty_confound_r: 0.412\ndifficulty_dif_correlation: -0.34\n\nresult_rationale: Steps 1-2 replicate cleanly under median tier — temporal DIF on GSM8K (C%=18.9%) and semi-synthetic ΔTPR (+0.818)\n\nNow I'm looking at Step 3 more carefully. The raw correlation still shows the effect (r=−0.478), but item difficulty is muddying the picture — DIF-C items tend to be harder, and harder items naturally show bigger gaps between models that memorized easy content versus those that didn't, regardless of whether they're marked as DIF. Once I account for both accuracy and difficulty, the partial correlation weakens to −0.289 with a confidence interval that now touches zero, which suggests this is more exploratory evidence than a clean finding, especially with only 33 items.\n\nThe degrees of freedom are tight here — around 28 with three controls — so p-values are inflated and a reviewer would be right to flag that the upper bound of the confidence interval includes zero. This shifts the result from a main finding to supplementary evidence.\n\nNow I'm moving into the bifactor IRT equating pilot. The model partially converges on about 56% of subjects, explains 41% of variance through the general factor, and achieves reasonable fit indices, though it hasn't quite hit the equating target yet.\n\nLooking at the replacement scenarios, the equated reliability drops as I increase the percentage of items replaced — from 0.982 at 5% down to 0.908 at 31% — and it fails to reach the 0.95 target once replacement hits 20% or higher. The calibration error sits at 0.31. The bifactor approach captures MMLU's multidimensional structure across 57 subjects only partially; smaller subjects like virology with just 166 items don't converge well, and the CFI of 0.87 signals the model is still underfitting the data, which makes sense given the structural complexity.\n\nWithin the subjects that do converge, anchor items perform well with 92.3% staying DIF-free, and at low replacement rates the equating works reliably. But at the 31% replacement level that matches the actual proportion of DIF-C items in the data, I'm falling short of the 0.95 target. The RMSE of 0.31 means the generated items are only roughly aligned to the originals in difficulty. This confirms the bifactor approach is a useful starting point but needs a more sophisticated multidimensional IRT model with domain-specific factors to handle MMLU's heterogeneity properly. I'm moving this to the appendix as a methodological pilot.\n\nNow looking at the item generation calibration plan, I generated 400 items with 312 passing quality checks — moderate success but the difficulty predictions remain imprecise.\n\nThe LLM-generated items achieve a 78% quality pass rate with decent construct alignment at 0.81 and cognitive level matching at 0.73, but the difficulty calibration is problematic. Prompt-based difficulty targeting only reaches ρ=0.32, which aligns with what the literature shows, though I'm still well below my 0.75 target. The student simulation approach performs better at 0.58, and the holdout panel correlation of 0.61 suggests the items have some validity, but the overall difficulty RMSE of 0.42 indicates substantial calibration error.\n\nThe item discrimination metric of 0.87 is respectable but falls short of the 1.0 target, meaning the generated items are slightly less discriminative than the originals. The core issue is that MMLU's broad domain coverage and 4-choice format make it harder to simulate student performance accurately compared to more constrained assessment contexts. This is a below-benchmark result for the median tier, so I'm treating this as a negative pilot — the generation pipeline works mechanically but doesn't produce psychometrically sound items for valid equating. I'll drop this from the main claims and include it as a brief appendix note motivating future work on constrained generation with iterative calibration.\n\nNow looking at the Bayesian cumulative DIF approach. The simulation shows steady power gains across administrations — starting at 0.41 for a single administration and climbing to 0.68 across four, though it falls just short of the 0.70 target at three administrations (reaching 0.62). The false positive rate is well-controlled at 0.048, and prior sensitivity between flat and informative priors is minimal at 0.06, suggesting the results are robust. Computation is reasonable at 12 minutes, but the assumptions underlying the Bayesian model are fairly strong.\n\nThe gap between simulated and target power reflects using realistic MMLU parameters rather than idealized conditions from prior work. The modest prior sensitivity indicates that while the method is stable, informative priors from earlier administrations don't provide much additional benefit. Since this is purely simulation-based without real multi-administration LLM benchmark data, it's more of a confirmatory result showing evidence accumulation works in theory rather than a major contribution. This belongs in the appendix as a direction for future work.\n\nFor the graceful degradation analysis, I'm examining how performance holds up when items are replaced, with a correlation of 0.994 at 5% replacement — a clean degradation curve but supplementary to the main findings. Looking at these metrics, the replacement analysis shows that random item swaps degrade ranking stability smoothly until hitting a critical point around 27% replacement where ρ drops below 0.95 — just shy of the actual 31% DIF-C rate. This is useful context for understanding safe replacement thresholds, though it's a simulation that depends on the actual calibrated items from the earlier plans to have real practical value. Construct coverage degrades faster than stability because domain-specific items can't be randomly substituted without losing representation.\n\nNow moving to the item-focused trees analysis, which complements the categorical DIF work but doesn't substantially alter the core findings. The continuous release date approach identifies 4,102 items with significant DIF versus 3,918 from the categorical Mantel-Haenszel method, with a Jaccard overlap of 0.74 showing substantial agreement despite some disagreement in which items flag.\n\nThe IFT-unique items tend to capture gradual, monotonic DIF patterns that cross thresholds at non-standard boundaries around September 2023, while MH-unique items show sharp step-function patterns that the tree structure smooths over. About 8.3% of items display nonlinear DIF trends that neither method handles well. This confirms MH's robustness to cohort definition choices while revealing that some instability manifests gradually and some abruptly—a supplementary check rather than a primary finding, so it belongs in the appendix.\n\nNow moving to architecture stratification, which appears to be a non-issue at the median tier. (99.98% decoder-only). The dataset is almost entirely decoder-only models (5,226 out of 5,227), so removing that single outlier doesn't change the analysis at all—the Jaccard similarity stays at 1.0 and the temporal confounding percentage remains at 31.3%. The IRT diagnostics don't improve in this homogeneous subset because the real problem was never architectural diversity; it's MMLU's inherent 57-subject multidimensionality that causes the misfit. Nearly all models show extreme standardized residuals (98.1% with |Lz|>2), which reflects this structural limitation rather than any mixing of architectures. This finding actually serves a defensive purpose in the paper—it neutralizes a potential reviewer concern about architecture confounding while being honest that the IRT issues are fundamental and will require multidimensional modeling to resolve. I'm including this as a robustness check in the main text.\n\nNow I'm planning the semi-synthetic contamination validation. I'm injecting controlled amounts of contaminated items into a clean subset and tracking how detection performance changes across different contamination levels and temporal windows. The results show baseline false positive rates around 31%, with true positive rates climbing from 22% at low contamination up to 67% at higher levels, while false positive inflation stays minimal in cross-subject matching scenarios.\n\nThe key finding is that the dose-response relationship holds cleanly (R²=0.97), confirming the method has genuine detection power when contamination is actually present. More importantly, I'm discovering a critical methodological distinction: cross-subject matching prevents false positive inflation entirely, but within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable itself, inflating false positives by about 19%. This is a novel insight about how the matching strategy fundamentally affects what we're actually measuring.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what tables and figures this experiment should produce, and I need to write up the analysis sections. I'm also pulling together the median tier metrics to complete the full specification—nonuniform DIF percentages around 11%, Mantel-Haenszel agreement around 0.71, and the partial correlation bounds I'm already using.\n\nWriting the full YAML specification...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18248,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18248},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"sessi